{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And I returned</b> (ושבתי). As if saying: I turned back and returned from this matter — I had thought that it is good for a person to rejoice — [but he cannot] rejoice, because there is oppression in the world. His property is taken from him by force, and he is oppressed — whether a prince or a judge who takes a bribe, or a thief, oppresses him. The meaning of <i>from the hand of their oppressors</i> (מיד עושקיהם) is power: there is power in the hand of the oppressors, while the oppressed have no strength, only to weep, and they have no comforter. For the way of a mourner weeping over the dead is that comforters come to him and he is consoled. He mentioned <i>and they have no comforter</i> (ואין להם מנחם) twice because his weeping and his outcry, time after time, are of no avail."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:27.218815954Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}